## Queue history

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## How the desk works from now on

1. Find candidates using subject or semantic similarity; neither establishes a duplicate.
2. Read the body, look up the tenant locale and booking-form version, then identify the channel.
3. Merge only when the observed behavior and verified context support the same cause.
4. Keep unknown tenants, unexplained timing shifts and conflicting evidence separate for investigation.
5. Retain original IDs and provenance. Update the issue's count from distinct report IDs.
6. Recheck this page when tenant forms, date parsing or the live queue change.
   Assign a review owner and date; this snapshot must not silently become a live count.

Identical subjects can represent different causes: Alder Physio form versus app;
Copper Studio form versus Morrow Tax form.
Different subjects can share a cause: Kettle Books app versus email;
Pale Harbour form versus Alder Physio form.
An unknown sender has no verified tenant context and must not be automatically merged.

## Consequences and handoff

Two independent engineering tickets replace the single-fix interpretation. Their closure reasons follow the later course engineering notice: parser completed; hint not planned because booking-v3 was retired.
This review does not mutate the historical ticket-system export or claim that reports have
already been moved in the real desk. The two engineering tickets linked above are published in the challenge repository.
The reproduction checks above describe the original problem paths. Engineering closure does not individually explain the anomalous bodies; retain them for follow-up if symptoms recur.

